  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 427 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nWe remain in southwest flow aloft for now with an upper trough \nmoving out of Manitoba and into Ontario early this morning while the \nridge remains dominant over the southeast US. Sfc low pressure is \ncentered over western KS with its associated cold front extending \nfrom northwest KS up through south central and southeast Nebraska. \nAs the front slowly works its way into the area today, thunderstorm \nchances increase into north central and northeast KS this morning, \nalthough coverage looks to be limited initially. Convergence along \nthe front should strengthen by mid to late afternoon, helping to \nprovide some better lift for scattered storms, a few of which could \nbe strong to severe into the early evening. The front looks to make \nit to around a line from Abilene to Manhattan to Oskaloosa by peak \nheating. Moisture pooling along the boundary should aid in \ndeveloping CAPE to 3000-4000 J/kg south of the front with more \nmodest shear around 25-30 kts. Given these parameters with inverted-\nV profiles, think damaging wind is the primary hazard with any \nstronger updraft that could get going and then collapse. As for \ntemperatures, north central and far northeast KS likely only see \nhighs in the 70s today, even falling into the 60s this afternoon \nwith clouds and rain moving in sooner. Those areas along and south \nof the aforementioned line will have more time to heat up and should \nsee highs in the 80s to low 90s before storms eventually develop.\n\nHeading into tonight, the system evolves into a heavy rain and \nflooding threat. The sfc front should stall somewhere around \nsoutheast KS with isentropic lift over the boundary into our area, \nand the LLJ helping to feed moisture and sustain storms into our \narea as well. Model guidance has been consistent in placing the \nmoisture axis right over the CWA with Pwat between 2-2.3\", which is \nwell over max values for mid-September. The 00z CAM guidance seems \nto highlight the area of the going Flood Watch well, with several of \nthe HREF members showing swaths of 3-5\" in far northeast KS, even \nhigher in more extreme cases. The RRFS has been less bullish on that \nidea and the 06z runs of the HRRR and NAM Nest have backed off on \nthose higher magnitude amounts as well, but the 06z has trended a \nbit further south with the 2-3\" amounts. In any case, think an inch \nor two is a solid bet for most places given the setup, while 3\" with \nlocally higher amounts would be possible in locations that see \nmultiple rounds. Opted to leave the location of the Flood Watch as \nis, but if more CAM guidance suggests heavier rain could end up \nfurther south, an expansion may be needed.\n\nRain should come to an end by mid-day Wednesday as the LLJ weakens \nthrough the morning and upper level perturbations get pushed north \nof the area. The upper ridge in the southeast US looks to retrograde \nthrough the end of the week, which would push the trajectory of any \nembedded waves aloft north of the area and give us a chance to dry \nout. This also favors WAA through the end of the week, leading to \nafternoon temperatures returning to the 80s and lower 90s. \n\nA shortwave moving from the Intermountain West across the northern \nRockies brings our next chance for rain this weekend with a more \nmeaningful change in air mass behind the system. Ensemble \ndifferences in timing and strength of the system keep likely PoPs in \nsome part of the forecast area Friday night through early Sunday \nmorning, but given the differences there should be plenty of dry \ntime within that time frame. There is better agreement on cooler \ntemperatures early next week with highs in the 70s.",
